    fix(node-bus-worker): decode the wire directive member text_b64 (CF-354)
    
    The scheduler sends the dispatch directive as `text_b64` (base64 of the UTF-8 text) because
    crier's control-key guard refuses prose that quotes JSON key names. `scripts/node-bus-worker.py`
    read only `dispatch.get("text")`, so every dispatch the scheduler actually sends was refused by
    this repository's own node with the named `brief` row, and the live fleet only worked because a
    dispatcher outside this repository decodes the member. `specs/ENVELOPE.md` called `text` mandatory
    and never described the encoding.
    
    Worker: new `directive_text(dispatch) -> (text, reason)` used by `process()` in place of the bare
    `dispatch.get("text")`. `text_b64` wins when present and is decoded as UTF-8; a `text_b64` that
    does not decode (bad base64, non-UTF-8, or empty/whitespace after decode) is the named `text_b64`
    refusal and NEVER falls back to another member, because a corrupted dispatch must not run a
    different instruction than the one that was sent. Only when `text_b64` is absent does the worker
    fall back to `task`, `text`, `prompt`, `message`, `body` (first non-empty string wins), matching
    the sender-side fallback list in docs/MASTER-DOC-SCHEMA.md §3c. `brief.digest` is still sha256 over
    the directive text, which is now the DECODED text, so a digest taken over the base64 is refused.
    The absent-instruction refusal keeps its wording (`carries no `text`` / reason `brief`), and
    nothing prints the directive.
    
    Spec: `specs/ENVELOPE.md` now states the rule instead of leaving `text` undefined — decode
    `text_b64` first and never consult another member while it is present, a decode failure is a named
    refusal and not a silent fallback, the fallback order for older senders, and that `brief.digest` is
    sha256 over the decoded directive text; the example dispatch carries `text_b64`.
    
    Tests: new `tests/test_bus_dispatch_encoding.py` (unittest, hermetic — one `mktemp -d`, no network,
    no credential) asserts what the harness RECEIVED, not what the worker said it would do. Measured,
    `python3 tests/test_bus_dispatch_encoding.py` rc=0:
    
      PASS  A1  text_b64-only dispatch runs; the harness receives exactly the decoded bytes
      PASS  A2  a dispatch carrying only the legacy `text` still runs (no regression)
      PASS  A3  corrupt text_b64 -> named `text_b64` refusal, no fallback, harness never started
      PASS  A4  no instruction member -> the existing named `brief` refusal, unchanged
      PASS  A5  brief.digest over the base64 (not the decoded text) is refused by `brief_digest`
    
    The same fixture pointed at the pre-fix source (CF_BUS_WORKER_SOURCE=<5b11bdc worker>
    CF_BUS_DISPATCH_PREFIX_CONTROL=1) prints
    
      PASS  P0  the PRE-FIX source refuses a text_b64 dispatch with the `brief` row (the red)
    
    and the control prints FAIL against the fixed worker, so it discriminates rather than merely
    passing.
    
    selftest: section 54 runs the fixture (one PASS row) and the red control against
    `git show 5b11bdc:scripts/node-bus-worker.py` (one PASS row); an unavailable pre-fix source is a
    named FAIL row, not a pass. `bash tests/selftest.sh` ends `SELFTEST: PASS` (exit 0) with 384 PASS
    rows and 59 FAIL rows, every FAIL a negative fixture — gateway_start refusals, board-lint and
    landing refusals — and section 54 contributes none. docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md gains the §54 mapping
    row and its current-state census moves to 54 numbered sections + 3 lettered = 57 arms
    (`bash docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh` prints arms_plan=57 arms_tree=57, RESULT: OK), which
    §39 grades.
    
    Not pushed: the foreman pushes and verifies remote parity.
